When parents are helping their children plan their application for RMIT (Royal Melbourne Institute of Technology, Australia's top practical public university), it is recommended to confirm the following key information with the school official (International Admissions Office + Target College) around the four core dimensions of **curriculum adaptation, portfolio threshold, employment exit, and student protection** to avoid pitfalls:

---

### 1. Core information of the course (determines academic qualifications, graduation difficulty and adaptability)
1. **Academic certification and value**
 - Whether the target major is certified by the corresponding industry association in Australia (such as EA certification for engineering, AACA certification for architecture, DIA certification for design, CPA certification for accounting) will directly affect subsequent career development and immigration qualifications;
 - Whether the course is full-time offline and fully meets the certification requirements of the Chinese Ministry of Education Study Abroad Service Center (excluding the risk that some mixed and short-term part-time courses cannot be certified);
 - Whether there is an honors degree (Honours) channel for undergraduates/masters, the GPA threshold for applying for honors degrees, the additional academic length, and the value for subsequent further study/employment. 
2. **Course Flexibility and Thresholds**
 - Are there any prerequisite subject requirements (for example, science and engineering require high school physics/mathematics, and design requires art foundation)? If the child does not have a relevant background, can it be supplemented through preparatory/international freshman/bridge courses;
 - Rules for changing majors and subdivisions after enrollment: credit deduction ratios for changing majors in the same field, GPA requirements, and application time limits;
 - Academic system and semester start arrangements: How many semesters start each year, whether admission can be deferred, and whether there are any policies related to accelerated graduation and gap breaks. 
3. **Practice and Assessment Difficulty**
 - The duration of the mandatory (compulsory) internship/practical project, the arrangement method (assigned by the school/self-application), whether it is paid, and what the corresponding cooperative companies are (RMIT is good at practice, but the resources of different majors vary greatly);
 - Assessment method: whether it is based on examinations or projects/papers/portfolios, the average passing rate of international students, re-examination and retake policies and fees after failing the course. 
4. **Campus and teaching**
 - The teaching campus of the target major (RMIT has multiple campuses such as City, Brunswick, Bundoora, etc., with large differences in location, transportation, and surrounding living costs), and whether there are differences in teachers and professional facilities at different campuses; 
 - Teaching mode: mainly offline or mixed online and offline, proportion of small classes (Tutorial), and teacher-student ratio. 

---

### 2. Portfolio requirements (core admission thresholds for majors such as art/design/architecture/media)
If your child applies for a major that requires a portfolio, be sure to confirm the details with the college (just looking at the official website brochure may not be clear enough):
1. **Basic submission rules**: Portfolio submission form (SlideRoom/Behance link/PDF), file size/number of pages/resolution limit, number of works requirements, whether there is a specified topic or free choice of topics;
2. **Review preference**: The school pays more attention to creative logic, research process, technical implementation ability, and completeness of finished product; whether it is necessary to attach a sketchbook (process draft), creation description, word count and content requirements of the creation description;
3. **Remedial path**: If the portfolio does not meet the standards, is there an opportunity to make up for it, or is there a preparatory/transition course (such as design preparatory course) that can be connected to make up for the portfolio requirements? What is the pass rate for the portfolio from preparatory to full-time courses? 
4. **Counseling resources**: Does the school have an official portfolio guide, reference for excellent portfolios from previous years, and online portfolio presentations/counseling activities for international students? 

---

### 3. Related to employment and development (the export issue that parents are most concerned about)
1. **Real employment data**: **International graduate employment rate** (3 months/6 months after graduation), average starting salary of the target major, please pay attention to confirm whether the data is for all students or only for international students (the employment data of local students and international students are very different);
2. **Employment service resources**: Is there any employment guidance specifically for international students (resume revision, mock interviews, industry matching teachers), which cooperative companies are there in the target major, are there exclusive industry job fairs and corporate presentations every year, and the coverage of the alumni network in the corresponding industry;
3. **Work visa and immigration adaptation**: Whether the major meets the application requirements for the Australian graduate work permit (485 visa), and how long the work permit is (for example, 2 years for a general undergraduate degree, 3 years for a taught master's degree, and it may be extended for shortage majors); if there is an immigration plan, whether the occupation corresponding to the major is on the skilled immigration list, and whether the course meets the academic requirements for career assessment;
4. **Employment support for returning students**: Does the school have a domestic employment service network, which domestic companies it cooperates with, and whether there are job fairs or domestic alumni communities for returning international students. 

---

### 4. Student support and protection (relevant to children's adaptation, safety and academic success)
1. **Enrollment and life adaptation**: Are there free airport pick-ups, temporary accommodation arrangements, is there Chinese guidance during the freshman week; is the application difficulty, cost, and room type of school dormitories, and is there counseling and contract review services for off-campus rentals;
2. **Academic Assistance**: Is there free academic English tutoring (essay writing, presentation skills, etc., targeting the shortcomings of international students), is there peer mentoring, and is there an early warning and make-up intervention mechanism for students who fall behind;
3. **Physical and physical health and safety**: Are there free psychological counseling services and are there Chinese psychological counselors? Are there 24-hour security and late-night escort services on campus? Are there clinics on campus and can they assist in using OSHC overseas student health insurance?
4. **Financial and visa support**: Application conditions, amounts, and deadlines for admission scholarships, selection rules for subsequent academic excellence scholarships; limits on the legal working hours for international students, whether the school has on-campus job resources; whether there are dedicated visa consultants to help answer questions related to visa renewal and work permit application. 

---

### Tips
RMIT's course requirements and Australia's work permit/immigration policies may be adjusted every year. It is recommended to give priority to the latest information through the **RMIT International Admissions Office, the official email of the target college, the school's China representative office, and the official online seminar**, and do not rely solely on second-hand information from intermediaries.
